# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) for local development.

# --- Cloudflare Access service token ----------------------------------------
# Only needed where `pnpm dev` cannot open a browser -- CI, or an AI agent
# session. `wrangler.jsonc` marks the EMAIL binding `remote: true`, so the dev
# server opens a proxy session against the deployed Worker, which sits behind
# Access. On an interactive terminal the Vite plugin falls back to
# `cloudflared access login` instead and these two are unnecessary.
#
# Create one under Zero Trust > Access > Service auth > Service Tokens, then
# add it to the Worker's Access policy with the action "Service Auth" -- a
# token that is not on the policy answers 403.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/access-controls/service-credentials/service-tokens/
#
# Editing these does not hot-reload; restart the dev server after a change.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=your-token-id.access
CLOUDFLARE_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=your-token-secret

# Runs every binding locally and skips the remote proxy session, so neither the
# service token nor `cloudflared` is needed. `send_email` stops reaching the
# real binding, so mail is not delivered.
# CLOUDFLARE_VITE_FORCE_LOCAL=true

# --- Cloudflare Access JWT validation ---------------------------------------
# Production only: the Worker skips validation under `import.meta.env.DEV`
# (workers/app.ts), so these are inert locally and must be set as Worker
# secrets for a deploy. Both values come from the one-click Access modal under
# Settings > Domains & Routes. TEAM_DOMAIN may be the base Access URL or the
# full /cdn-cgi/access/certs URL.
POLICY_AUD=your-access-policy-audience-tag
TEAM_DOMAIN=https://your-team.cloudflareaccess.com
